  ]
 },
 "signs": [
  {"x": 12, "y": 19, "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Northern Rock grew from a building society into a big mortgage bank, based in Gosforth. In September 2007, when the markets it borrowed from dried up, savers queued outside its branches to take their money out: the first run on a British bank in well over a century. It was nationalised in February 2008, months before the worldwide crash that autumn.", "kind": "plaque", "dx": 0.475, "plaque": "econ_northern_rock", "title": "Northern Rock"},
  {
   "x": 6,
   "y": 9,
   "plaque": "newcastle_regina",
   "title": "Regina and Barates",
   "text": "At Arbeia, the Roman fort at South Shields, a tombstone found in 1878 remembers Regina, a freedwoman of the Catuvellauni, a British tribe, who died aged 30. Her husband Barates came from Palmyra in Syria, and he added a line in his own Palmyrene Aramaic: Regina, freedwoman of Barates, alas. Roman Tyneside was full of people from everywhere.",
   "people": "romans"
  },
  {
   "x": 6,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "newcastle_bede",
   "title": "Bede of Jarrow",
   "text": "Bede spent almost his whole life as a monk at Wearmouth and Jarrow, a few miles down the Tyne, and in 731 finished his Ecclesiastical History of the English People, the first history of the English. He also made counting years from the birth of Christ, AD, the normal way to date them.",
   "people": "anglo_saxons"
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "THE TYNE BRIDGE. Opened in 1928, the symbol of the city. Its builders, Dorman Long, went on to make the Sydney Harbour Bridge, which looks rather like it. Every Geordie has a favourite bridge, and it's often this one."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "GATESHEAD MILLENNIUM BRIDGE. Opened in 2001, the world's first tilting bridge. When a ship comes, the whole arch swings up like an eyelid, and it is nicknamed 'The Blinking Eye'. It really does blink."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "THE NEW CASTLE. Robert Curthose, son of William the Conqueror, built a wooden fort here in 1080, and the town is named for it. The stone keep dates from the 1170s. A railway line runs right past it."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "GREY STREET. Often voted one of the finest streets in England. It curves down to Grey's Monument, for the Prime Minister of the Great Reform Act of 1832. 'Meet you at the Monument' is the city's whole diary."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 46,
   "text": "THE TOWN MOOR. About 1,000 acres of open grass in the middle of a city. The Freemen of Newcastle may graze their cattle here, and some still do, which is why the cows have a better address than you."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 4,
   "text": "THE QUAYSIDE. In the Middle Ages this was the busiest bit of the city: coal, wool and salt left from here to everywhere. On Sunday mornings there's a market with cheese, crafts, and a sausage roll that could convert the Pope."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE SAGE. A great curving ripple of glass and steel, opened in 2004 and now called the Glasshouse, like a silver armadillo that learned music. The main concert hall is called Hall One. It is exactly what it says."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE BALTIC. Not a sea, a flour mill. Joseph Rank's flour company built it in 1950, and in 2002 it became a very large gallery for contemporary art. There is a viewing box on the roof, and the view is not contemporary at all."
  },
  {
   "x": 65,
   "y": 57,
   "text": "THE ANGEL OF THE NORTH. Antony Gormley's steel angel stands beside the A1, 20 metres tall with wings 54 metres wide, almost as wide as a jumbo jet's. She went up in 1998 and some people hated her. Nobody does now."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Robert Stephenson and Company, the world's first purpose-built locomotive works, opened in Newcastle in 1823.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "newcastle_stephenson",
   "title": "Stephenson and Company"
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 9,
   "plaque": "newcastle_st_nicholas",
   "title": "The Lantern Spire",
   "text": "St Nicholas' has a rare 15th-century lantern spire, a stone crown held up on flying arches, long used as a landmark by ships on the Tyne. The church became a cathedral in 1882. Nicholas is the patron saint of sailors, which suits a river town nicely."
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "newcastle_swan",
   "title": "Joseph Swan's Bulb",
   "text": "Joseph Swan, a chemist born in Sunderland, worked on the electric light bulb in Newcastle at the same time as Edison in America. His lamps lit the Lit and Phil library, and Mosley Street became the first street in the world lit by electric bulbs. The two rivals later went into business together."
  },
  {
   "x": 6,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "newcastle_blaydon",
   "title": "The Blaydon Races",
   "text": "'The Blaydon Races', written by Geordie Ridley in 1862, follows a merry crowd from Balmbra's in the Cloth Market, just here, to the races at Blaydon, with mishaps all the way. It is the region's unofficial anthem, sung at the football and at weddings alike."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "newcastle_greggs",
   "title": "Greggs",
   "text": "Greggs began in 1939 when John Gregg delivered eggs and yeast by bicycle around Gosforth. He opened a bakery, and his family grew it into a chain that now stretches the length of Britain. The sausage roll is a matter of local pride, and of local argument."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 37,
   "plaque": "arts_pitmen_painters",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Pitmen Painters",
   "text": "In 1934 a group of coal miners at Ashington, up the coast, asked the Workers' Educational Association for a class in art appreciation. The tutor, Robert Lyon, gave them paint instead, and the Ashington Group painted their own world for fifty years: the pit, the allotment, the whippet, the Sunday dinner. Their pictures hang at Woodhorn Museum, and Lee Hall's play The Pitmen Painters, from 2007, took their story round the world. Critics called them naive. They had been underground since they were fourteen."
  }
 ],
